    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Guarani is a Tupian language that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> kept: where people across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> shifted to the colonial languages, Paraguayans held on to Guarani while also adopting Spanish.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionaries first wrote it down, in grammars and a dictionary of 1629–1640; the Guarani of their missions built new words from its own roots, while the Guarani spoken outside took in Spanish words freely.</li><li>Banned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>’s state schools for over a hundred years, yet used by populists to stir national feeling, it was made equal to Spanish in the constitution of 1992.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Guarani marks time on nouns as well as verbs: tetã ruvichakue is “ex-president” and tetã ruvicharã “president-elect”, and pa'irãgue, “the ex-future priest”, is someone who studied for the priesthood but did not finish.</p></aside>
    </section>

